# Haml closes blocks by indentation, and the reader writes each close as a
# `}` in the markup before the next Ruby. Where that markup had too few
# bytes, the close spilled into the next block and closed it instead: two
# blocks ending at the left margin left the new `do |g|` closed at its body
# (5:5 a helper call, resolved, 1.0) and the old ones open. A `#{}` at the
# left margin had no byte at all, so the block before it stayed open over it
# and its parameter hid a helper call from --dead. With room for one close,
# the inner block closes there, and the outer (`f`) after the new block.
